<% content_for :title, "Hinduism & Bipolar | Bipolar Sober" %>

<main class="hinduism-bipolar-page">

  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="hinduism-bipolar-hero">

    <div class="hinduism-bipolar-hero-content">

      <p class="hinduism-bipolar-eyebrow">
        BIPOLAR SOBER / BIPOLAR / PERSPECTIVES / HINDUISM
      </p>

      <h1>
        <span>BIPOLAR</span>
        <span>THROUGH A</span>
        <span>HINDU LENS.</span>
      </h1>

      <p class="hinduism-bipolar-intro">
        YOUR MIND CHANGES.<br>
        YOU ARE MORE THAN ITS CURRENT STATE.
      </p>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 01 / THE SELF -->
  <section class="hinduism-section hinduism-pink">

    <div class="hinduism-section-heading">

      <p class="hinduism-number">
        01 / THE SELF
      </p>

      <h2>
        YOU ARE<br>
        NOT JUST<br>
        YOUR MIND.
      </h2>

    </div>

    <div class="hinduism-copy">

      <p class="hinduism-lead">
        Hindu traditions offer a powerful question:
        who are you underneath everything that changes?
      </p>

      <p>
        Your thoughts change. Your emotions change.
        Your energy changes. Your body changes.
        Your circumstances change.
      </p>

      <p>
        Bipolar disorder can make those changes feel enormous.
      </p>

      <p>
        One day your mind may feel unstoppable.
        Another day it may tell you that getting out of bed
        is impossible.
      </p>

      <p>
        In several Hindu philosophical traditions, especially
        Vedanta, the deepest self is called <strong>Atman</strong>.
        The changing contents of the mind are not the whole of
        that self.
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        MY MENTAL STATE IS SOMETHING I AM EXPERIENCING.
        IT IS NOT EVERYTHING I AM.
      </blockquote>

      <p>
        That doesn't erase bipolar disorder or make its symptoms
        unreal. It simply gives you another way to relate to them.
      </p>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 02 / GUNAS -->
  <section class="hinduism-section hinduism-yellow">

    <div class="hinduism-section-heading">

      <p class="hinduism-number">
        02 / THE GUNAS
      </p>

      <h2>
        THE MIND<br>
        HAS MODES.
      </h2>

    </div>

    <div class="hinduism-copy">

      <p class="hinduism-lead">
        Hindu philosophy describes nature through three qualities,
        or gunas: sattva, rajas, and tamas.
      </p>

      <div class="hinduism-gunas">

        <div>
          <span>SATTVA</span>
          <p>
            Clarity. Balance. Harmony. Awareness.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>RAJAS</span>
          <p>
            Activity. Restlessness. Desire. Intensity.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>TAMAS</span>
          <p>
            Heaviness. Inertia. Darkness. Resistance.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>

      <p>
        These are philosophical concepts, not psychiatric diagnoses.
        Mania is not simply "rajas," and depression is not simply
        "tamas."
      </p>

      <p>
        But the framework can still give us useful language for
        noticing the quality of our internal state without immediately
        turning that state into our identity.
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        WHAT KIND OF ENERGY IS MOVING THROUGH ME RIGHT NOW?
      </blockquote>

      <p>
        Sometimes naming the quality of an experience gives us enough
        distance to decide how we want to respond to it.
      </p>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 03 / WITNESS -->
  <section class="hinduism-section hinduism-black">

    <div class="hinduism-section-heading">

      <p class="hinduism-number">
        03 / THE WITNESS
      </p>

      <h2>
        WATCH<br>
        THE MIND.
      </h2>

    </div>

    <div class="hinduism-copy">

      <p class="hinduism-lead">
        Your mind can be loud as hell without every thought
        deserving your obedience.
      </p>

      <p>
        Some Hindu philosophical and contemplative traditions
        emphasize observing thoughts, sensations, and emotions
        rather than automatically identifying with them.
      </p>

      <div class="hinduism-example">

        <p>Instead of:</p>

        <h3>
          "I HAVE TO DO THIS RIGHT NOW."
        </h3>

        <p>Notice:</p>

        <h3>
          "MY MIND FEELS EXTREMELY URGENT RIGHT NOW."
        </h3>

      </div>

      <p>
        That distinction matters.
      </p>

      <p>
        An urge can be real without being an order.
        A thought can be intense without being accurate.
        A feeling can be overwhelming without being permanent.
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        OBSERVE FIRST.<br>
        THEN DECIDE.
      </blockquote>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 04 / DHARMA -->
  <section class="hinduism-section hinduism-purple">

    <div class="hinduism-section-heading">

      <p class="hinduism-number">
        04 / DHARMA
      </p>

      <h2>
        WHAT IS<br>
        YOUR JOB<br>
        RIGHT NOW?
      </h2>

    </div>

    <div class="hinduism-copy">

      <p class="hinduism-lead">
        Dharma is a huge concept, but one useful way to approach
        it is through responsibility, right action, and how we
        live according to our values and obligations.
      </p>

      <p>
        When everything feels overwhelming, you don't necessarily
        have to solve your entire fucking life.
      </p>

      <p>
        You can ask:
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        WHAT IS MINE TO DO IN THIS MOMENT?
      </blockquote>

      <p>
        Maybe it's taking medication as prescribed.
        Maybe it's sleeping.
        Maybe it's calling someone.
        Maybe it's apologizing.
        Maybe it's not sending the text.
        Maybe it's showing up for an appointment.
      </p>

      <p>
        The question shifts attention away from controlling
        everything and toward the next responsible action
        available to you.
      </p>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 05 / ATTACHMENT -->
  <section class="hinduism-section hinduism-pink">

    <div class="hinduism-section-heading">

      <p class="hinduism-number">
        05 / ACTION & OUTCOME
      </p>

      <h2>
        DO THE WORK.<br>
        RELEASE THE<br>
        OUTCOME.
      </h2>

    </div>

    <div class="hinduism-copy">

      <p class="hinduism-lead">
        The Bhagavad Gita famously wrestles with action,
        responsibility, and attachment to results.
      </p>

      <p>
        You can influence your actions.
        You cannot completely control what happens afterward.
      </p>

      <p>
        That's painfully relevant to recovery and mental health.
      </p>

      <p>
        You can take your medication.
        You can go to therapy.
        You can protect your sleep.
        You can avoid substances.
        You can learn your warning signs.
        You can repair relationships.
      </p>

      <p>
        And you still cannot guarantee that life will behave.
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        MY RESPONSIBILITY IS THE ACTION.<br>
        NOT TOTAL CONTROL OF THE RESULT.
      </blockquote>

      <p>
        Releasing attachment to an outcome doesn't mean
        not caring about it.
      </p>

      <p>
        It means refusing to make your peace entirely dependent
        on controlling something you cannot fully control.
      </p>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 06 / BALANCE -->
  <section class="hinduism-section hinduism-yellow">

    <div class="hinduism-section-heading">

      <p class="hinduism-number">
        06 / BALANCE
      </p>

      <h2>
        BORING CAN<br>
        BE FUCKING<br>
        BEAUTIFUL.
      </h2>

    </div>

    <div class="hinduism-copy">

      <p class="hinduism-lead">
        A life built around balance can feel pretty unimpressive
        when your brain is attracted to extremes.
      </p>

      <p>
        Sleep. Food. Routine. Medication. Rest.
        Showing up. Doing the next ordinary thing.
      </p>

      <p>
        None of it has the fireworks of an extreme state.
      </p>

      <p>
        But spiritual discipline doesn't always look dramatic.
        Often it looks repetitive.
      </p>

      <div class="hinduism-contrast">

        <p>CHAOS IS EXCITING.</p>

        <span>≠</span>

        <p>CHAOS IS FREEDOM.</p>

        <span>+</span>

        <p>STABILITY IS NOT FAILURE.</p>

      </div>

      <p>
        There can be something sacred in learning how to return
        to the ordinary.
      </p>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 07 / PRACTICE -->
  <section class="hinduism-section hinduism-black">

    <div class="hinduism-section-heading">

      <p class="hinduism-number">
        07 / PUT IT INTO PRACTICE
      </p>

      <h2>
        OKAY.<br>
        NOW WHAT?
      </h2>

    </div>

    <div class="hinduism-copy">

      <p class="hinduism-lead">
        You don't have to become Hindu to find useful questions
        inside Hindu philosophy.
      </p>

      <div class="hinduism-practice-list">

        <div>
          <span>01</span>
          <p>
            <strong>OBSERVE.</strong><br>
            What is my mind doing right now?
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>02</span>
          <p>
            <strong>DON'T CONFUSE STATE WITH SELF.</strong><br>
            This experience is happening to me.
            It is not the entirety of me.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>03</span>
          <p>
            <strong>CHECK THE ENERGY.</strong><br>
            Is my mind clear, restless, heavy, agitated,
            driven, or exhausted?
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>04</span>
          <p>
            <strong>ASK WHAT'S MINE TO DO.</strong><br>
            What is the next responsible action?
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>05</span>
          <p>
            <strong>DO THE ACTION.</strong><br>
            Focus on what you can actually choose.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>06</span>
          <p>
            <strong>LET GO OF TOTAL CONTROL.</strong><br>
            You can influence the outcome without owning
            the entire universe.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- ENDING -->
  <section class="hinduism-ending">

    <div>

      <p class="hinduism-ending-small">
        THE MIND CHANGES.
      </p>

      <h2>
        YOU ARE<br>
        <span>STILL HERE.</span>
      </h2>

    </div>

    <div class="hinduism-ending-copy">

      <p>
        Thoughts come and go.
      </p>

      <p>
        Emotions rise and fall.
      </p>

      <p>
        Energy changes.
      </p>

      <p>
        The work is not controlling every movement of the mind.
        It is learning how to observe what is happening,
        act according to your values, and remember that no
        single mental state contains the whole of who you are.
      </p>

      <%= link_to "← BACK TO PERSPECTIVES",
          bipolar_perspectives_path,
          class: "hinduism-back-button" %>

    </div>

  </section>

</main>